# Fhe Advanced

_Reference for zama-solidity skill (zama-protocol plugin)_

# Raw FHE and Public Settlement

Use the installed [FHE.sol](https://github.com/zama-ai/fhevm/blob/main/library-solidity/lib/FHE.sol) and [API docs](https://docs.zama.org/protocol/solidity-guides/smart-contract/functions.md) for supported types and overloads. Repository `main` may be ahead of the published package.

- **Persist only what survives the transaction.** New result handles need `FHE.allowThis` for later contract use and `FHE.allow` for intended users. Use `FHE.allowTransient` for same-transaction forwarding. Accepting an existing handle requires checking the caller's permission too.
- **Preserve encrypted control flow.** `FHE.select` takes matching encrypted types; both arguments are computed. Arithmetic wraps, narrowing truncates, and shifts reduce the shift count modulo the bit width. Preserve explicit range/overflow and rounding rules.
- **Check the operation set.** `eaddress` is an encrypted address type, not a general arithmetic integer. `euint256` does not support the full smaller-integer arithmetic set. Array `sum`/`isIn` overloads exist in published 0.13.4; check installed-version support and release costs before replacing loops.
- **Public decryption is a staged flow.** Mark the intended handles with `FHE.makePubliclyDecryptable`, request public decryption offchain, then verify the proof with `FHE.checkSignatures` against the frozen expected handles in order. Advance the application stage once; never accept caller-selected handles as settlement evidence. Re-mark new handles after state updates when disclosure is still intended.
- **Tests and production differ.** `FHE.decrypt` is not a production API. Functional mocks validate logic and permissions; they do not establish ciphertext readiness or load capacity.

Read the [public-decryption guide](https://docs.zama.org/protocol/solidity-guides/smart-contract/oracle.md) and `code-map.md` for verified settlement examples. Decryption may lead to economic actions after proof verification; revealing a value is a privacy decision.
